# Ethernet Frame

An **Ethernet frame** is the format used to send data over a **Local Area Network (LAN)**.

## Ethernet Frame Structure

| Field | Size | Description |
|-----|-----|-------------|
| Preamble | 7 bytes | Synchronizes the receiver |
| Start Frame Delimiter | 1 byte | Indicates the start of the frame |
| Destination MAC Address | 6 bytes | Address of the receiving device |
| Source MAC Address | 6 bytes | Address of the sending device |
| Type/Length | 2 bytes | Identifies the protocol used |
| Data | 46–1500 bytes | Actual transmitted data |
| Frame Check Sequence (FCS) | 4 bytes | Error detection |

## Purpose of Ethernet Frame

The Ethernet frame helps network devices to:

- Identify the **source device**
- Identify the **destination device**
- Detect **transmission errors**

---

# Key Definitions

**Encapsulation**

> The process of adding headers and trailers to data as it moves through network layers before transmission.

**De-encapsulation**

> The process of removing headers and trailers from received data at the destination device.

**Ethernet Frame**

> A structured data unit used at the **Data Link Layer** to transmit data within a LAN.
